# THE BOTTOM OF A SUM'S DESCENT. One row per reached layer that is not itself built from parts,
# with the route that reached it -- so a leaf on two branches appears twice.
#
# ⛔⛔ IT IS NOT THE BOTTOM OF EVERY DESCENT, AND WHICH BOTTOM APPLIES DEPENDS ON THE QUANTITY
#    RATHER THAN ON THE GRAPH. A sum over parts bottoms out here, because below an F-terminal
#    layer there is nothing left to add. A REMAINDER bottoms out EARLIER, at the first node
#    whose own `n` and `d` were not both scaled by one factor with width, because differencing
#    them is legitimate there and the filed figure is a claim its composer stands behind.
#    composition/unsettled.sqlc is that predicate and composition/settled_remainders.sqlc is
#    that walk. A leaf satisfies it trivially, which is why this relation looks like the
#    general case and is not.
#
# ⭐⭐ A LEAF IS DEFINED BY WHAT IT IS NOT, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS AN ANTI-JOIN AGAINST A NAMED
#    RELATION RATHER THAN A `NOT EXISTS` INSIDE A PREDICATE. composition/fusions.sqlc is
#    "the layers that are assembled"; a reached layer missing from it is a layer somebody
#    measured directly. Naming the positive relation makes the negation readable and gives
#    the same set a second use elsewhere.
#
# ⛔ COUNT THE ROWS PER (ROOT, LEAF) AND ANYTHING ABOVE ONE IS THE SAME SUPPLY ENTERING ONE
#   TOTAL TWICE. Both routes are in the `path` column, which is what makes the finding
#   actionable instead of merely true.
# ⚠️ AN ANTI-JOIN THAT MATCHES NOTHING RETURNS THE WHOLE LEFT SIDE AND LOOKS LIKE A WORKING
#   RELATION. Nothing about the row count says the exclusion happened.
#   ⭐ THE LAW IS RECOMPUTED RATHER THAN RECORDED, algebra/leaves.sqlc, every run. It is also
#   the one law with a live counter-example: rewrite this file as `EXCEPT` and it reports
#   28 = 15 + 5, because descent is a bag on the key and EXCEPT deduplicates eight rows away.
#
# ⭐⭐ `f.filing` IS THE RIGHT COLUMN TO TEST, AND THE CHOICE IS NOT FREE. It is the join key and
#    `pm.part.composition` is NOT NULL, so a matched row can never carry a NULL there. Testing a
#    NULLABLE column of the right-hand relation instead would keep matched rows whose payload
#    happens to be null -- an anti-join that quietly under-removes, with no symptom.
-- asrt:Part followed to a layer that names no parts of its own.
SELECT d.*
FROM      (
    :compose(composition/descent.sqlc)
) d
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(composition/fusions.sqlc)
) f
       ON f.filing = d.filing AND f.layer = d.layer
WHERE f.filing IS NULL
